  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nWednesday and Thursday both look dry and seasonally warm as surface \npressure noses in and slowly drifts east. With relatively light \nnortherly flow in place both days, lows will generally be in the \n50s, a few 60s on Wednesday morning, with highs in the lower to mid \n80s.\n\nThursday night into Friday weaker upper level ridging moves through \nas a trough of low pressure deepens over the Northern Rockies. This \ntrough will continue to dig into the weekend, then expected to \nspread across the Canadian border later Sunday into Monday. \n\nThis pattern change to southwest flow aloft continues into next \nweek and will likely bring periods of showers and thunderstorms.\nThe best chance for now looks to be Friday night as the upper \nlevel ridge is shoved east and warmer and more moist airs builds\nin. Initially Friday the area looks too capped to produce showers\nand thunderstorms. Maybe some ACCAS activity on Friday morning \nbut even that looks like a low probability.\n\nThe main piece of energy with this initial push moves through Friday \nnight and should bring scattered showers and thunderstorms. For now \nthe severe weather threat appears to be fairly low. Another wave \nmoves into the Western High Plains on Saturday which should bring \nenhanced heating ahead of a weak front. This could be a brief period \nof thunderstorms and isolated severe, but too far out to determine \njust yet. Moderate shear and minor to moderate instability are \npossible. \n\nSunday into Monday gets a bit messier with some timing difference on \nthe exiting and lifting northeast of the main upper level jet. The \nmodels suggest that the right entrance region will exit to the \nnortheast sometime Sunday night into Monday afternoon which could \nserve as our next better chance for showers and thunderstorms.",
